      <h2>Small tools I've built</h2>

      <h3>kind-local-test-suite</h3>
      <p>A one-command local Kubernetes test bed: a multi-node <a href='https://kind.sigs.k8s.io/'>kind</a> cluster whose Flux components are managed by the Flux Operator, with secrets kept SOPS-encrypted in git. It's for trying out GitOps changes locally, then recreating the cluster from scratch when it gets messy. This one lives in a private repo.</p>
      <p><small>2025 &middot; Shell</small></p>

      <h3><a href='https://github.com/liskl/pre-commit-kustomize'>pre-commit-kustomize</a></h3>
      <p>A <a href='https://pre-commit.com/'>pre-commit</a> hook that runs <code>kustomize build</code> inside a container running as a non-root user, so a broken overlay fails before it gets committed. github.com is added to the image's known_hosts, which lets overlays use remote refs. It started from an existing open-source kustomize image, and also bundles the KSOPS plugin for overlays that carry SOPS-encrypted secrets.</p>
      <pre>repos:
- repo: https://github.com/liskl/pre-commit-kustomize
  rev: v0.0.7
  hooks:
  - id: kustomize
    args: [path/to/your/overlay]</pre>
      <p><small>2023 &middot; Dockerfile</small></p>

      <h3><a href='https://github.com/liskl/whoami'>whoami</a></h3>
      <p>A tiny Go demo app that answers HTTP requests with the pod's hostname and serves Prometheus metrics on <code>/metrics</code>. Scale it to a few replicas, hit it a few times, and you can see whether load balancing is actually spreading the traffic.</p>
      <p><small>2022 &middot; Go</small></p>

      <p>Most of what I build now runs on the homelab cluster; see the <a href="/server.html">homelab page</a>.</p>

